## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of synchronization primitives and memory ordering in a toy OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Barrier reuse via a generation counter
- Race-free wakeups with a condition variable analogue
- Memory visibility and minimal locking
- Testing for progress and fairness under contention

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int count;
  int needed;
  int gen;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
  pthread_cond_t cv;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) {
  b->count = 0;
  b->needed = n;
  b->gen = 0;
  pthread_mutex_init(&b->m, NULL);
  pthread_cond_init(&b->cv, NULL);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&b->m);
  int g = b->gen;
  if (++b->count == b->needed) {
    b->count = 0;
    b->gen++;
    pthread_cond_broadcast(&b->cv);
  } else {
    while (b->gen == g) {
      pthread_cond_wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&b->m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for dynamic participant counts?
- How would you test for spurious wakeups and ensure progress under contention?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the candidate's understanding of file descriptor semantics, shared underlying objects, and race-free updates to per-process tables. It also assesses error handling and edge case coverage in a kernel interface.

## Key Concepts

- Per-process file descriptor table management
- Reference counting for shared file objects
- Atomic close-on-update semantics
- Robust input validation and error reporting

## Code Example

```c
int dup2(int oldfd, int newfd) {
    if (oldfd < 0 || oldfd >= 8 || newfd < 0 || newfd >= 8) {
        return -1;
    }
    if (oldfd == newfd) {
        return newfd;
    }

    lock_fd_table();
    if (newfd is open) {
        decrement_ref_count(newfd);
        clear_fd_entry(newfd);
    }
    copy_fd_entry(oldfd, newfd);
    increment_ref_count(newfd);
    unlock_fd_table();
    return newfd;
}
```

## Testing Approach

Test cases should cover: valid fd pairs with various reference counts, the oldfd == newfd case, boundary conditions (0 and 7), invalid oldfd, and newfd out of range. Also test dup2(-1, 5) returning -1, dup2(5, -1) returning -1, and scenarios where newfd is already open versus closed.

## Why This Is Asked

Multi-tenant I/O contention tests practical OS scheduling design, fairness guarantees, and real workload handling on a block device.

## Key Concepts

- per-tenant queues
- credit-based throttling
- burst handling
- fairness metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal sketch: enqueue/dequeue with per-tenant credits (pseudo-C)
class TenantQueue {
  constructor(creditLimit) {
    this.credits = creditLimit;
    this.queue = [];
  }
  enqueue(req) { this.queue.push(req); }
  dequeue() { return this.queue.shift(); }
}
function chooseNext(tenants) {
  // pick queue with max deficit ratio: (creditUsed / max(credit,1))
  let best = null; let bestScore = -Infinity;
  for (const t of tenants) {
    const score = t.credits <= 0 ? -Infinity : 1 - t.credits / t.creditLimit;
    if (score > bestScore) { bestScore = score; best = t; }
  }
  return best && best.queue.length ? best.dequeue() : null;
}
```

